+## Private field-page preparation
+
+`HybridPropertyPageImage` prepares neutral native SlottedPage images, with one explicit allocation owner for page, record-copy and serialized buffers. It packs group, column and variable extension records behind stable PageId/slot/slot-generation references; fixed directories can share the existing canonical map writer. It is not an installed pool/format, does not allocate IDs, and must never be persisted as an ordinary ROW page. Metadata must assign the logical data IDs before map-key planning in the same eventual publication. No new generic variable B+Tree map is retained. A private variable-key experiment was not adopted: payload-bearing native separators create a mechanism/capacity cost, not a measured latency result.
+
+Keep shared-pool physical generation separate from semantic commit generation: data pool reuse generations do not advance on every graph commit. A column at semantic generation 7 can validly live in a data pool of physical generation 1, provided its group/descriptor/snapshot semantic bounds are valid. The descriptor metadata-frame physical generation follows its separate metadata publication rules. Private clone/overwrite preserves stable slot identities; erase/reuse advances slot generation. These image checks do not replace native graph routing, final authority/integrity, resource scratch admission, allocation/retirement or R3.2 full lifecycle acceptance.
+
+## Ordinary graph publication spine
+
+`NativeGraphHybridPropertyRootMutation` is a typed late-root request carrying expected database/root identity, replace/clear mode and eight namespace IndexIds. It adds no generic graph callback or physical authority. The writer validates the expected database/root and authenticates a present old descriptor before preparing graph data. Late preparation resolves each requested tree from this candidate's final roots or the retained current directory, rejects absent/retired/aliased namespaces and uses the actual batch generation. The descriptor and ordinary Index data then use the same unchanged data fence → inactive A/B → superblock fence → acknowledgment. Ordinary requests need no bulk scratch; the existing bulk early hook retains its scratch contract.
+
+The isolated build currently publishes empty descriptors or clears only; nonempty descriptor preparation still rejects, including after ordinary Index pages have been prepared, and the old graph remains authoritative. No production caller stages these requests. This is the publication spine, not installed field pools, conversion or full native property routing. Early data-ID assignment still needs the metadata-owned field coordinator before locator-key planning; never manufacture a standalone ID commit or bulk scratch merely to use the existing early hook.
+
+The bounded conversion classifier and reverse projection now have one storage-owned implementation, `HybridPropertyGroupConversion.hpp`. It consumes authenticated, immutable ROW roots borrowed from the enclosing coordinator. Its one fixed workspace allocation is charged through a required retained PMR owner (no default-resource fallback); the 64-owner/4096-membership/85-column bounds and the <=1 MiB workspace bound remain unchanged. Source roots/heap values, output buffers and native publication have separate allocation owners. Cancellation, quota refusal and invalid input release the workspace without changing ROW sources. No IDs, fields or roots are published by this planner. The benchmark `ReservedGroupConversion` is now only a laboratory view adapter using this implementation, with its additional view storage separately charged; production storage does not include benchmark or query execution headers. The metadata-owned early field coordinator must use this native planner before final locator/map planning; this migration alone is not R3.2 acceptance.
+
+Private early ID preparation is now owned by `HybridPropertyConversionPreparation` in metadata. Its constructor is accessible only to the native metadata store and a test accessor, not ordinary graph callers. It requires the plan's semantic generation to equal the actual batch generation, obtains logical PageIds from that same `MetadataWriteBatch` while packing append-only group/column/inline-extension records, and emits final owner/column/extension/group slot locators. It does not allocate physical pools, publish maps/fields/IDs or call a generic planning callback. Neutral physical epoch 1 is only a private image placeholder; later owner binding must install the actual pool epoch. On cancellation/refusal the enclosing batch must be discarded; local consumed IDs are not an independently published reservation. Output images, locator buffers and escaped record copies retain their resource owner after source roots/plan release. The final coordinator must create the shared <=1 MiB conversion subaccount under the existing QUERY/WRITE parent; this helper cannot infer an arbitrary PMR resource's quota. Oversized inline values refuse; independent large-reference creation/authentication/retirement is still pending. The ordinary metadata entry now invokes this private preparation under the typed request below, then refuses before physical publication; the backend/public transaction layer still stages no such requests. Generic-pool/nonempty-descriptor guards remain.
+
+## Private field-owner binding
+
+The isolated format reserves PageOwnerKind 16 for HYBRID_PROPERTY; 13–15 remain retired adjacency codes and must always be rejected. Reuse SGK_PROPERTY_PAGE_POOL without adding a new segment kind. `bindPrivateOwner` consumes only a validated private image: require a sealed PROPERTY catalog entry, exact pool owner/physical generation, exact expected page/LSN, zero flags and the full SlottedPage payload width. Enumerate all live slots with the retained allocation account, validate their typed group/column/extension shapes and semantic visibility, and only then rebind without allocation. Failure leaves the image unchanged and retryable. Large-reference envelope validation still needs caller admission; it does not authenticate content or prove global ownership.
+
+This is private binding only. Generic pool read matching and every standalone allocate/append/replace/evacuate entry reject HYBRID_PROPERTY, even in the isolated build. Do not permit field publication until the metadata-owned typed coordinator, all field readers/writers, integrity and exact retirement support the same atomic lifecycle. ROW frames cannot stand in for field frames. Pinned tree authentication remains required separately from the descriptor codec; the codec now correctly permits independent positive pool epochs above metadata/semantic generations while retaining all metadata visibility checks.
+
+## Ordinary metadata conversion source and early preparation
+
+`NativeGraphHybridPropertyConversion` carries Node/Edge kind, one group ID, a retained resource, cooperative cancellation, a permitted physical reservation range and a positive field-pool owner. It never supplies selected frames or logical identities. It is an optional typed part of the descriptor request. The initial mode permits only a standalone group conversion: REPLACE, all caller replacement IndexIds zero, no ordinary Entity/Property/LargeValue/derived/ID-lease mutations, and a required exact expected metadata generation. The later coordinator owns final map/root binding. The entry requires a MaterializedByteBudget whose used+available maximum is at most1MiB; a missing/oversized/unknown resource rejects before source reads. The actual parent QUERY/WRITE allowance remains authoritative.
+
+Readonly Entity/Property/LargeValue adapters attach before taking the writer; actual reads use its pinned current database/generation/descriptor root. First authenticate at most64 primary Entity Map entries and exact typed records (the last native group has63 IDs), count authenticated ROW fields and reject totals above4096 before decoding any value. Include live propertyless owners. Then materialize only those owners through the retained Property cursor with existing PropertyDecodeWorkspace admission. Keep all sources immutable/alive through native classification and private preparation, using actual batch generation and IDs. Never accept caller payloads as authoritative or construct a separate ID-only publication.
+
+Current code deliberately throws UNSUPPORTED_VERSION after private page and dense fixed-map planning succeeds, before physical pools/final maps/descriptor publication. Failure preserves the original file/root/high-water marks and refunds owned buffers/source heaps, while retaining unrelated caller credit. LargeValue-backed ROW sources are read/authenticated by the existing store; oversized native inline fields refuse rather than truncate or reuse a whole multi-field ROW reference as a field. Independent field LargeValue production is still absent. Replace this explicit refusal only together with physical placement, final native maps, full graph routing/index/overlay/ownership/integrity/recovery/retirement and both conversion directions. Tests of this refusal are early-coordinator evidence, not successful conversion or R3.2 acceptance.
+
+
+The ordinary coordinator now uses the existing BulkBTreeBuilder for its four role-specific directory plans. Authenticate absence of every reserved role, including an empty result. Encode each prefix plus final locator/source suffix as a key-only leaf record. Empty directories allocate no tree. The repeatable ordered sources retain the immutable prepared field object and its resource, so they do not borrow caller rows or the classifier. Index pool ownership uses the namespace IndexId; the supplied field-pool owner is separate. Metadata still selects physical slots and assigns final tree PageIds later.
+
+All bulk planning vectors and nested separator/child buffers now retain OwnedStdVector admission, shared planning nodes/control blocks use makeSharedOwned, and the native initial-plan callback and pool vector have explicit ownership. Admitted semantic-CRC fresh backing is released after its bytes. The native preparer restores its retained allocation scope on invocation. This accounts planner state; downstream physical store/batch buffers and complete allocation/retirement costs must still be audited before publication. Do not treat planning as successful field persistence or claim that whole publication now fits the staging cap. Evidence: 20261019-r3-directory-plans, six necessary modules, default60/60 and isolated84/84.
